  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday)\nIssued at 938 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nWe will end the work week with the passage of an upper wave early\nFriday morning, with some uncertainty as to how much moisture \nadvection will happen ahead of this system. The GFS is a bit more \naggressive with moisture and would result in some light rain \nduring the early hours on Friday, while the ECMWF is drier and \nwould likely only result in increasing clouds. For this reason we \nwill stick with blended guidance capping PoPs near 20%, and any \nrainfall that does occur should begin clearing by late Friday \nmorning. Dry weather will follow as high pressure spreads \nsouthward from the Midwest in the wake of a cold front. High temps\nwill drop to the upper 60s/lower 70s behind this front with \nnorthwesterly winds bringing in cooler and drier air. We should \nalso see clouds thinning through the morning hours on Friday \nresulting in pleasant fall conditions by Friday afternoon.\n\nOverall the weekend looks nice as zonal flow persists aloft. High\ntemps will reach the low to mid 60s each day with overnight lows \nin the 30s to lower 40s. In fact, we will likely see our first \nfrost of the season in some locations, especially those north of \nthe TN River and in the higher terrain where current forecast \ntemperatures drop to 32 degrees. This will be a trend to watch \nover the coming days, but there are still fairly significant \ndifferences regarding the upper and mid level patterns in long \nterm guidance. Models do agree that by early next week upper flow \nwill shift to the north as a stronger trough axis shifts east over\nthe area, reinforcing high pressure and keeping dry, near \nseasonable conditions over the TN Valley.",
